# Book C: Windows (cyber operations)
DECKS = []

DECKS.append(("C1", "Windows Ch.1 - Command Prompt, PowerShell, Environment Variables", [
("mc","What does the Windows command prompt do on startup, and what else does it keep?",
 ["It executes commands stored in the registry on startup and records its history","Nothing on startup and keeps no history","It loads PowerShell modules","It reads /etc/profile"],0,
 "1.8: The command prompt executes commands on startup that are stored in the registry; it also records its history."),
("mc","Which older scripting technologies can still manage Windows?",
 ["Batch scripts and VBScript","Bash and Perl","Python and Ruby","AppleScript"],0,
 "Ch1: Windows users can still use the older batch scripts and VBScript to manage Windows systems."),
("mc","How is PowerShell described?",
 ["The modern, extendable approach to scripting in Windows, with several versions, and it now runs on Linux","A deprecated shell","A GUI-only tool","Windows-only with one version"],0,
 "Ch1: PowerShell is the modern, extendable approach to scripting in Windows. There are several versions and it now can be used on Linux."),
("mc","PowerShell can be extended to use which technologies?",
 [".NET or the older COM, DCOM, and COM+ objects; modules can be downloaded and incorporated","Only batch files","Only WMI","Only VBScript"],0,
 "Ch1: PowerShell can be extended to use .NET or the older COM, DCOM and COM+ objects. PowerShell modules can be downloaded and incorporated."),
("mc","How many methods does PowerShell have for its command history?",
 ["Two, plus users can also have PowerShell keep a complete transcript","One","Three, with no transcript","None"],0,
 "Ch1: PowerShell has two different methods for its command history, and users can also have PowerShell keep a complete transcript."),
("mc","How can PowerShell commands be obfuscated?",
 ["By passing commands as Base64 encoded strings or by using downloaded PowerShell modules","By renaming the executable","By compiling them to .NET","They cannot be obfuscated"],0,
 "Ch1: PowerShell commands can be obfuscated, either by passing commands as Base64 encoded strings or by using downloaded PowerShell modules."),
("mc","Where are Windows environment variables stored and managed?",
 ["In different scopes, managed from the command line, PowerShell, and graphical tools","Only in the registry, by regedit only","Only in PowerShell profiles","Only at install time"],0,
 "Ch1: Windows stores environment variables in different scopes; they can be managed from the command line, from PowerShell, and from graphical tools."),
("sa","Which registry value makes the command prompt run commands at startup, and why do attackers like it?",
 "The AutoRun value under HKCU\\Software\\Microsoft\\Command Processor (or the HKLM equivalent) - every cmd.exe launch runs it, giving quiet user-level persistence that runs under a trusted process.",
 "Added key point for the 1.8 registry-startup takeaway."),
("sa","Name PowerShell's two history mechanisms and the transcript feature, and say which survives a reboot.",
 "Get-History / the session history buffer (in memory, lost when the session closes) and PSReadLine's ConsoleHost_history.txt (written to the user profile under AppData\\Roaming\\Microsoft\\Windows\\PowerShell\\PSReadLine, survives reboot). Start-Transcript / Stop-Transcript writes a full transcript of input and output to a file.",
 "Added key point - the two-methods takeaway is a likely exam item."),
("sa","Which PowerShell switch runs a Base64 command, and why is it an attacker favorite?",
 "powershell -EncodedCommand <base64 of the UTF-16LE script>, usually with -NoProfile -WindowStyle Hidden -ExecutionPolicy Bypass. It hides keywords from simple command-line string matching and survives quoting problems in droppers.",
 "Added key point about obfuscation."),
("sa","Name the three environment variable scopes and how to set a variable in each.",
 "Process (current session): set VAR=value in cmd, or $env:VAR='value' in PowerShell. User and Machine (persistent): setx VAR value [ /M ], the System Properties GUI, or [Environment]::SetEnvironmentVariable('VAR','value','User'|'Machine').",
 "Added key point for the scopes takeaway."),
("sa","Why is the PowerShell execution policy not a security boundary?",
 "It only stops accidental running of script files; it is trivially bypassed with -ExecutionPolicy Bypass, piping a script into powershell -Command, or encoding the command, so it stops mistakes, not attackers.",
 "Added key point."),
("sa","Which command shows the command prompt's history and which keys recall it?",
 "doskey /history (F7 shows a history popup, F8 searches, arrow keys recall). Note cmd history lives only in the session.",
 "Added key point."),
]))

DECKS.append(("C2", "Windows Ch.2 - Users and Groups", [
("mc","What are Windows users and groups used for?",
 ["To control the access, rights, and privileges of users","Only for display names","Only for licensing","Only for file sharing"],0,
 "2.10: Users and groups are used by Windows to control the access, rights, and privileges of users."),
("mc","What identifies each Windows user and group?",
 ["A security identifier (SID); some are well-known and identical across systems and domains, others are local","A UID number","A username only","A GUID that changes daily"],0,
 "2.10: Each user and group has an associated SID. Some are well-known and are the same across systems and domains; others are local to a system or a domain."),
("mc","Where is local user account data stored?",
 ["In the security accounts manager (SAM) database on that system","In Active Directory","In /etc/passwd","In the registry hive HKCU only"],0,
 "2.10: Local users access resources on a single system; data for these accounts is stored locally in the SAM database."),
("mc","Where is domain user data stored, and what access does it give?",
 ["In the Active Directory data store; access across a domain and into trusting domains and forests","In each workstation's SAM; access to one system","In /etc/shadow","In the registry of the DC only, for that DC"],0,
 "2.10: Domain users can be used to access resources across a domain and into trusting domains and forests. Data for domain users is stored in the Active Directory data store."),
("mc","Which users are named as having special roles on Windows?",
 ["Administrator, NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM, the guest account, and machine accounts","root and daemon","Admin and User","Only Administrator"],0,
 "2.10: Several users have special roles on Windows systems, including Administrator, NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM, the guest account, and machine accounts."),
("mc","What is the difference between local groups and domain groups?",
 ["Local groups assign privileges on a local system; domain groups assign privileges across a domain","They are the same thing","Local groups work across forests","Domain groups only exist on workstations"],0,
 "2.10: Local groups are used to assign privileges to a local system. Domain groups are used to assign privileges across a domain."),
("mc","If a domain group and a local group share a name, what is true?",
 ["They are still different groups (even with the same name and SID), and domain group membership does not grant membership in the corresponding local group","They are automatically merged","The local group wins","Membership is inherited both ways"],0,
 "2.10: Domain groups are different than local groups, even in cases where they have the same name and SID. Membership in a domain group does not grant membership in the corresponding local group."),
("sa","What is a well-known SID? Give two examples.",
 "A SID that is identical on every Windows system or domain. Examples: S-1-5-18 = NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM, S-1-5-32-544 = the built-in Administrators group, S-1-1-0 = Everyone, S-1-5-11 = Authenticated Users. Account-specific SIDs end in a RID: 500 = Administrator, 501 = Guest, 512 = Domain Admins.",
 "Added key point for the SID takeaway."),
("sa","What is a machine account and why does it matter?",
 "Every domain-joined computer has its own account in AD (named COMPUTER$) with a password it rotates automatically; it is how the machine authenticates to the domain, and an attacker with SYSTEM on the host can act as that machine account.",
 "Added key point."),
("sa","Which commands enumerate local and domain users and groups?",
 "net user, net user /domain, net localgroup, net group /domain; PowerShell Get-LocalUser, Get-LocalGroup, Get-LocalGroupMember, and the AD module's Get-ADUser, Get-ADGroup, Get-ADGroupMember.",
 "Added key point."),
("sa","What is NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM and how does it compare to Administrator?",
 "A built-in local account (S-1-5-18) used by the operating system and most services; it has complete local control - more local power than Administrator - but it is not a domain identity: on the network it presents as the computer's machine account.",
 "Added key point."),
]))

DECKS.append(("C3", "Windows Ch.3 - Logons, Tokens, UAC, and Hashes", [
("mc","What does a user receive when they log on to Windows?",
 ["A login ID and one or more access tokens","A Kerberos-only ticket and nothing else","A SID that changes each logon","A shell only"],0,
 "3.6: There are several ways a user can log on to a Windows system. When a user logs on, they receive a login ID and one or more access tokens."),
("mc","What does UAC do and what governs it?",
 ["It allows users to use privileged tokens; the process is governed by registry policies set from group policy or the local security policy","It encrypts files; governed by BitLocker","It scans malware; governed by Defender","It manages services; governed by the SCM"],0,
 "3.6: User Account Control (UAC) allows users to use privileged tokens; the UAC process is governed by policies set in the registry, which can be modified either from group policy or the local security policy."),
("mc","How do modern Windows systems record password hashes, and where?",
 ["Using NTLM, in the local SAM and in Active Directory's data store (ntds.dit)","Using SHA-512 in /etc/shadow","Using bcrypt in the registry hive HKCU","Using LM only, in the boot sector"],0,
 "3.6: Modern Windows systems record password hashes using NTLM. These are stored in the SAM and in ntds.dit in Active Directory."),
("mc","What is true about access to the stored hashes?",
 ["They are protected, and even NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM cannot directly access them","Any user can read them","Only the network can read them","They are stored in plaintext"],0,
 "3.6/Ch3: These are protected, and even NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM cannot directly access them."),
("mc","How can the hashes be obtained despite that protection?",
 ["With Windows tools that can access them from Administrator or Backup Operator accounts","Only by physically removing the disk","They cannot be obtained","Only by resetting the password"],0,
 "Ch3: There are Windows tools that can access these hashes from Administrator or Backup Operator accounts."),
("mc","What can attackers do with the resulting hashes?",
 ["Crack them with John the Ripper, or use specialized tools and use the hashes in place of passwords","Only delete them","Only view usernames","Nothing without the salt"],0,
 "Ch3: Attackers can use tools like John the Ripper to crack the resulting password hashes or use specialized tools and use the hashes in place of passwords."),
("mc","What are Windows privileges and rights?",
 ["A collection given to users that gives fine-grained control over what the user can do","Only file permissions","Only group names","Only registry keys"],0,
 "Ch3: Windows users are given a collection of privileges and rights, which give fine-grained control over what the user can do."),
("mc","What is said about determining which users are currently logged on?",
 ["There are several methods, though all have limitations","There is exactly one reliable method","It is impossible","Only the DC can tell"],0,
 "Ch3: There are several methods to determine which users are currently logged on to a system, though all have limitations."),
("sa","What is pass-the-hash and why does it work?",
 "Using a captured NTLM hash directly to authenticate as the user without ever cracking it, because NTLM challenge-response proves knowledge of the hash, not the plaintext password. Specialized tools (psexec-style, NetExec/CrackMapExec, Metasploit's SMB modules with SMBPass set to the hash) accept a hash where a password would go.",
 "Added key point - pairs with the 'use the hashes in place of passwords' bullet."),
("sa","Name the methods for listing logged-on users and the limitation of each.",
 "query user / quser (interactive sessions on that host only), query session, psloggedon (Sysinternals, shows local and remote/resource-connected users, needs remote registry), WMI/CIM Win32_LoggedOnUser and Win32_ComputerSystem.UserName (shows the console user or stale sessions), net session (SMB connections only), and the Security event log (4624 logon events, with logon type). None sees everything - service, network and cached sessions differ.",
 "Matches the chapter exercise comparing enum_logged_on_users, system.exe, WMI, query user, psloggedon."),
("sa","What is the difference between a privilege and a right in Windows, and how do you list your own?",
 "A right (logon right) governs how you may log on (interactively, as a service, over the network); a privilege governs system-wide actions once logged on (SeDebugPrivilege, SeBackupPrivilege, SeTakeOwnershipPrivilege). whoami /priv lists privileges, whoami /groups lists group memberships and integrity level.",
 "Added key point."),
("sa","Name three Windows logon types and where you'd see them.",
 "Type 2 interactive (at the console), type 3 network (SMB/file share), type 10 RemoteInteractive (RDP), type 5 service, type 4 batch/scheduled task. They appear in Security event ID 4624.",
 "Added key point for 'several ways a user can log on'."),
("sa","Which process holds credential material in memory, and why do Administrator and Backup Operator accounts matter?",
 "LSASS holds credentials in memory; dumping it or the SAM/SYSTEM hives yields hashes. Administrator can enable SeDebugPrivilege and read LSASS or save the hives; Backup Operators hold SeBackupPrivilege, which lets them read protected files (including the hives) regardless of the DACL - that is why it is effectively an administrative group.",
 "Added key point."),
("sa","Why is a UAC consent prompt not a security boundary in Microsoft's model?",
 "A split-token administrator can elevate at will, and auto-elevating Microsoft binaries plus registry/COM tricks allow UAC bypasses; UAC is designed to prevent silent or accidental elevation by software, not to contain a determined attacker already running as that admin user.",
 "Added key point."),
]))

DECKS.append(("C4", "Windows Ch.4 - Files", [
("mc","How can Windows files be addressed?",
 ["Using full case-insensitive names or the older 8.3 format","Case-sensitively only","Only by inode","Only by GUID"],0,
 "4.8: Windows files can be addressed using their full case-insensitive names or in the older 8.3 format."),
("mc","Which file attributes and metadata are named in 4.8?",
 ["Attributes that mark files as hidden or read only, and several time stamps","Only the archive bit","Only ACLs","Only the owner"],0,
 "4.8: Files can have attributes that mark them as hidden or read only. Windows files have several time stamps."),
("mc","With what can Windows files be managed?",
 ["File Explorer, command-line tools, and PowerShell","File Explorer only","Only PowerShell","Only the registry editor"],0,
 "Ch4: Windows files can be managed with File Explorer, with command-line tools, and with PowerShell."),
("mc","Which special file and directory are named?",
 ["The hidden desktop.ini file in each directory and the directory C:\\Users\\Public","autoexec.bat and C:\\Temp","boot.ini and C:\\Windows","hosts and C:\\ProgramData"],0,
 "Ch4: Some files and directories in Windows have special purposes, including the hidden desktop.ini file in each directory and the directory C:\\Users\\Public."),
("mc","Which statement about links is correct?",
 ["Hard links are created between files, junctions between directories, and symbolic links by administrators can point to relative or network locations","Junctions link files","Hard links work across machines","Symbolic links cannot point to directories"],0,
 "Ch4: Windows shortcuts are files that point to other places in the file system. Hard links can be created between files, and junctions can be created between directories. Symbolic links can be created by administrators and can refer to relative locations and network locations."),
("mc","What NTFS feature allows one file to have more than one data stream?",
 ["Alternate data streams","Hard links","Junctions","Sparse files"],0,
 "Ch4: NTFS supports the use of alternate file streams, which allow one file to have more than one data stream."),
("mc","What are alternate data streams used for by Windows itself?",
 ["The Mark of the Web, which tracks files downloaded from the Internet","Disk compression","File encryption","Indexing only"],0,
 "Ch4: These are used in the Mark of the Web, which is how Windows tracks files downloaded from the Internet."),
("mc","Why do attackers deliver payloads inside .iso or .vhd files?",
 ["Files with the Mark of the Web receive additional scrutiny, and these file system containers avoid the mark","They compress better","They are faster to download","They cannot be scanned at all"],0,
 "Ch4: Because files with the Mark of the Web receive additional scrutiny, attackers use file system containers like .iso images and .vhd files to avoid the mark."),
("sa","Name the four NTFS timestamps and the term for faking them.",
 "Modified, Accessed, Created (Born), and MFT Entry modified - MACE/MACB. Faking them is called timestomping; the $STANDARD_INFORMATION times are easy to alter while $FILE_NAME times in the MFT are harder, which is how analysts catch it.",
 "Added key point for 'several time stamps'."),
("sa","How do you view and remove an alternate data stream / Mark of the Web?",
 "dir /r shows streams; Get-Item -Stream * or Get-Content -Stream Zone.Identifier reads it; Unblock-File or Remove-Item -Stream Zone.Identifier removes it (right-click Properties > Unblock in the GUI). The MOTW stream is named Zone.Identifier and contains ZoneId=3 for Internet.",
 "Added key point."),
("sa","Which command creates hard links, junctions and symbolic links, and who may create each?",
 "mklink /H link target (hard link, files, same volume), mklink /J link target (directory junction, local volumes), mklink /D link target (directory symbolic link) and mklink link target (file symbolic link). Symbolic links normally require administrator rights (or Developer Mode).",
 "Added key point."),
("sa","What is an 8.3 short name, and how is it a security issue?",
 "The legacy MS-DOS name such as PROGRA~1 for 'Program Files'. It gives an alternate path to the same file, so filters, allow-lists and quoting assumptions based on long names can be bypassed; it can be disabled with fsutil 8dot3name.",
 "Added key point."),
("sa","Why does copying a downloaded file to FAT32 remove the Mark of the Web?",
 "MOTW lives in an NTFS alternate data stream; FAT32 has no ADS support, so the stream (and therefore the mark) is lost - the same reason archives, ISOs and VHDs can strip or avoid it.",
 "Matches the chapter exercise in the photo."),
]))

DECKS.append(("C5", "Windows Ch.5 - Access Control", [
("mc","Which objects have a security descriptor?",
 ["Securable objects like files, directories, registry items, and processes","Only files","Only processes","Only registry keys"],0,
 "5.10: Securable objects like files, directories, registry items, and processes have a security descriptor."),
("mc","What are the four components of a security descriptor?",
 ["The SID of the owner and primary group, the DACL, the SACL, and a collection of flags","User, role, type, level","Read, write, execute, deny","Owner, group, world, other"],0,
 "5.10: The SID of the owner and primary group, the DACL for the object to control access, the SACL of the object to control logging, and a collection of flags."),
("mc","What is the difference between a DACL and a SACL?",
 ["The DACL controls access; the SACL controls logging/auditing","The DACL logs; the SACL controls access","They are the same","DACL is for users, SACL is for groups"],0,
 "5.10: DACL controls access, SACL controls logging."),
("mc","What is said about viewing security properties in File Explorer?",
 ["It can be used, but the interface has some subtle issues","It is the most accurate tool","It cannot show permissions","It shows SACLs only"],0,
 "5.10: File Explorer can be used to see the security properties of a file or directory, but the interface has some subtle issues."),
("mc","Which tools view and manage DACLs?",
 ["PowerShell (Get-Acl/Set-Acl) and the command line tool icacls","Only File Explorer","regedit and reg","sc and net"],0,
 "5.10: Users can view and manage DACLs in PowerShell and with the command line tool icacls."),
("mc","Which Sysinternals tools view security properties of objects?",
 ["AccessChk and AccessEnum","Process Explorer and Autoruns","PsService and PsExec","TCPView and Procmon"],0,
 "5.10: The Sysinternals tools AccessChk and AccessEnum can be used to view security properties of objects."),
("mc","What is SDDL?",
 ["A way to write access control rules in a string format","A logging protocol","A disk format","A scripting language for services"],0,
 "5.10: The SDDL language is a way to write access control rules in a string format."),
("mc","Who can take ownership of protected files and alter them?",
 ["Administrators or users with SeTakeOwnershipPrivilege or SeRestorePrivilege","Any authenticated user","Nobody","Only NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM"],0,
 "5.10: Windows protects some files against alteration even by NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM; however, administrators or users with the SeTakeOwnershipPrivilege or the SeRestorePrivilege can take ownership of these files and can then alter them."),
("mc","Which attack uses that ownership capability, and what defends against it?",
 ["The 'Sticky Keys' attack; Windows Resource Protection is designed to protect against these kinds of attacks","Pass-the-hash; UAC defends","Kerberoasting; BitLocker defends","Timestomping; Defender defends"],0,
 "5.10: Attackers can use this as part of a 'Sticky Keys' attack. Windows Resource Protection is designed to protect against these kinds of attacks."),
("sa","Describe the Sticky Keys attack step by step.",
 "Take ownership of C:\\Windows\\System32\\sethc.exe (or utilman.exe/osk.exe) and grant yourself write access, then replace it with cmd.exe (or set a Debugger value for it under Image File Execution Options). At the locked logon screen, pressing Shift five times launches the accessibility binary - now a SYSTEM command prompt - giving pre-authentication SYSTEM access and persistence.",
 "Added key point explaining the 5.10 bullet."),
("sa","Why does a deny ACE normally win over an allow ACE?",
 "Windows evaluates ACEs in order and stops at the first match; the canonical ordering puts explicit deny ACEs before explicit allow ACEs (and explicit before inherited), so an explicit deny is reached first.",
 "Added key point."),
("sa","Give the icacls and PowerShell commands to view and change permissions and ownership.",
 "icacls <path> shows the DACL in SDDL-like form; icacls <path> /grant User:(F) /deny /remove changes it; icacls /setowner and takeown /f <path> change ownership. In PowerShell: Get-Acl, then modify and Set-Acl.",
 "Added key point."),
("sa","Who owns protected Windows system files, and which tool repairs them?",
 "TrustedInstaller (NT SERVICE\\TrustedInstaller) owns them under Windows Resource Protection; sfc /scannow and DISM restore altered system files.",
 "Added key point."),
]))

DECKS.append(("C6", "Windows Ch.6 - The Registry", [
("mc","How is the registry organized?",
 ["As keys, which have subkeys and values that contain data","As files and directories only","As a relational database","As XML documents"],0,
 "6.5: The registry is organized as keys, which have subkeys and values that contain data."),
("mc","Where are registry keys stored?",
 ["Most are stored in hive files in the file system; some keys are volatile and built as the system runs","All in RAM only","All in C:\\Users","In Active Directory"],0,
 "6.5: Most registry keys are stored in the file system in hive files; some keys are volatile and built as the system runs."),
("mc","What is true about registry keys and values as objects?",
 ["They are securable objects with an associated DACL plus additional registry-specific permissions","They have no permissions","They use Linux-style rwx bits","Only hives have permissions"],0,
 "6.5: Registry keys and values are securable objects with an associated DACL. This is like the DACL for files with additional permissions specific to registry keys and values."),
("mc","How can registry permissions be modified?",
 ["With graphical tools and with PowerShell","Only by editing hive files directly","Only with icacls","They cannot be modified"],0,
 "6.5: These can be modified with graphical tools and with PowerShell."),
("mc","Which tools work with the registry?",
 ["Registry Editor (graphical), the reg command, and PowerShell with .NET","Only regedit","Only reg","Only Group Policy"],0,
 "6.5: The graphical tool is Registry Editor. Users can use the reg command, PowerShell with .NET, and PowerShell to work with the registry from a command line."),
("sa","Name the five registry root keys and what each holds.",
 "HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE (HKLM) system-wide settings; HKEY_CURRENT_USER (HKCU) the current user's settings; HKEY_USERS (HKU) all loaded user hives; HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT (HKCR) file associations and COM registration, a merged view of HKLM\\Software\\Classes and HKCU\\Software\\Classes; HKEY_CURRENT_CONFIG (HKCC) the current hardware profile.",
 "Added key point for the 6.5 structure takeaway."),
("sa","Where do the hive files live on disk, and which hive is a user's?",
 "System hives (SYSTEM, SOFTWARE, SAM, SECURITY, DEFAULT) are in C:\\Windows\\System32\\config; each user's hive is NTUSER.DAT in their profile directory (and UsrClass.dat in AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\Windows). HKLM\\HARDWARE is the classic volatile key built at boot.",
 "Added key point for the hive-file/volatile takeaway."),
("sa","Name three registry persistence locations attackers use.",
 "HKLM or HKCU \\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run and RunOnce; Winlogon Shell/Userinit values; Image File Execution Options Debugger values; services under HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services; and the cmd.exe AutoRun value from Chapter 1.",
 "Added key point."),
("sa","Give the reg and PowerShell commands to read, write and delete a registry value.",
 "reg query \"HKLM\\Software\\Foo\" /v Bar, reg add ... /v Bar /t REG_SZ /d value /f, reg delete ... /v Bar /f, plus reg save/load/export/import. PowerShell: Get-ItemProperty, New-ItemProperty/Set-ItemProperty, Remove-ItemProperty, with the HKLM: and HKCU: drives (Get-ChildItem HKLM:\\Software).",
 "Added key point."),
("sa","Name common registry value types.",
 "REG_SZ (string), REG_EXPAND_SZ (string with environment variables), REG_MULTI_SZ (multiple strings), REG_DWORD (32-bit number), REG_QWORD (64-bit), REG_BINARY (raw bytes).",
 "Added key point."),
("sa","Why is Base64 data in the registry a malware red flag?",
 "Fileless malware stores its encoded payload in a registry value and a small launcher decodes it with [System.Convert]::FromBase64String and runs it in memory, leaving nothing on disk for file scanning.",
 "From footnotes 19-20 in the photo (convert from Base64 to an array, then examine before executing)."),
]))

DECKS.append(("C7", "Windows Ch.7 - Processes", [
("mc","How are Windows applications, processes, and threads related?",
 ["Applications are programs running one or more processes that run one or more threads","A process runs one application only","Threads contain processes","They are the same thing"],0,
 "7.6: Windows applications are programs running one or more processes that run one or more threads."),
("mc","Which tools view and manage Windows processes?",
 ["Command line tools, Task Manager, Process Explorer, and PowerShell","Only Task Manager","Only PowerShell","Only Process Monitor"],0,
 "7.6: Windows processes can be viewed and managed with several tools, including command line tools, Task Manager, Process Explorer, and PowerShell."),
("mc","What do defenders examine, and what do attackers do about it?",
 ["Defenders look at the parent process; attackers have methods to obfuscate their parent","Defenders look at file size; attackers compress","Defenders look at the icon; attackers change it","Nothing is examined"],0,
 "Ch7: Defenders look at the parent process of processes being investigated; attackers have methods to obfuscate their parent."),
("mc","What governs the integrity of processes and securable objects, and where is it used?",
 ["Integrity levels, used in User Account Control (UAC)","Access tokens, used in Kerberos","SIDs, used in DNS","SACLs, used in BitLocker"],0,
 "Ch7: Processes and securable objects have an associated integrity; this is governed by integrity levels and is used in User Account Control."),
("mc","What should cyber operations professionals be familiar with?",
 ["The important Windows processes","Only third-party processes","Only the kernel","Only services"],0,
 "Ch7: Cyber operations professionals should be familiar with the important Windows processes."),
("sa","Name the Windows integrity levels from lowest to highest.",
 "Untrusted, Low (e.g., sandboxed browser tabs), Medium (standard user, and an admin's filtered token), High (elevated administrator), System (SYSTEM services and the kernel). A lower-integrity process cannot write to a higher-integrity object, which is the mechanism behind UAC.",
 "Added key point."),
("sa","Describe parent process ID (PPID) spoofing and how a defender spots it.",
 "A process is created with an explicit parent attribute (UpdateProcThreadAttribute PROC_THREAD_ATTRIBUTE_PARENT_PROCESS), so it appears to be a child of a benign process such as explorer.exe. Defenders compare process creation telemetry (Sysmon event 1 / 4688 with creator data), check start times and token/session mismatches, and notice impossible lineages such as winword.exe spawning powershell.exe.",
 "Added key point for the parent obfuscation bullet."),
("sa","List the core Windows processes and their expected parents.",
 "System (PID 4) -> smss.exe -> csrss.exe and wininit.exe (and winlogon.exe in each session); wininit.exe -> services.exe, lsass.exe, (lsm); services.exe -> svchost.exe instances and other services; winlogon.exe -> userinit.exe -> explorer.exe -> user applications. Red flags: an svchost.exe whose parent is not services.exe, a second lsass.exe, or any of these running from a path other than C:\\Windows\\System32.",
 "Added key point for 'be familiar with the important Windows processes'."),
("sa","Give the command-line and PowerShell commands to list and kill processes.",
 "tasklist (tasklist /svc, /m) and taskkill /PID n /F or /IM name /F; PowerShell Get-Process, Get-Process -Id, Stop-Process -Id n -Force, Get-CimInstance Win32_Process (shows ParentProcessId and CommandLine).",
 "Added key point."),
("sa","What is a boot trace in Process Monitor used for?",
 "Procmon can be configured to log activity from the very start of the next boot (Options > Enable Boot Logging), capturing file, registry and process activity that happens before you can log in - useful for finding early-launch persistence.",
 "Matches exercise 7-13 in the photo."),
]))

DECKS.append(("C8", "Windows Ch.8 - Services", [
("mc","What are Windows services?",
 ["Programs designed to run in the background without being launched by a user","Programs launched by double-clicking","Scheduled tasks only","Drivers only"],0,
 "8.3: Windows services are programs designed to run in the background without being launched by a user."),
("mc","How is a service controlled?",
 ["The service application interacts with a service control program, and users issue commands to that program to manage the service","Users signal the process directly","Only the kernel can control it","Through the registry editor only"],0,
 "8.3: The service application interacts with a service control program, and users can issue commands to the service control program to manage the service."),
("mc","Which tools manage Windows services?",
 ["Graphical tools, the command line tools sc and net, the Sysinternals tool PsService, and PowerShell","Only services.msc","Only sc","Only PowerShell"],0,
 "8.3: Windows services can be managed with several tools, including graphical tools, the command line tools sc and net, the Sysinternals tool PsService, and PowerShell."),
("sa","Name the service start types.",
 "Automatic, Automatic (Delayed Start), Manual (start on demand), Disabled, and Boot/System for drivers.",
 "Added key point."),
("sa","Name the three built-in service accounts in order of power.",
 "LocalSystem (NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM - full local power, machine account on the network), NetworkService (limited locally, machine account on the network), LocalService (limited locally, anonymous on the network). Services can also run as a named user or a managed service account.",
 "Added key point."),
("sa","Give the sc, net and PowerShell commands to query, start, stop and configure a service.",
 "sc query NAME, sc qc NAME (config, including binary path and account), sc start|stop NAME, sc config NAME start= auto, sc create/delete; net start NAME and net stop NAME; PowerShell Get-Service, Start-Service, Stop-Service, Set-Service -StartupType, New-Service, and Get-CimInstance Win32_Service for the path and account.",
 "Added key point."),
("sa","Name two service misconfigurations attackers abuse for privilege escalation or persistence.",
 "Unquoted service paths with spaces (Windows tries C:\\Program.exe first), weak file or directory permissions on the service binary (replace it), weak service DACLs allowing SERVICE_CHANGE_CONFIG (repoint binPath or change the run-as account), and simply creating a new service with sc create as an admin for SYSTEM-level persistence at boot.",
 "Added key point; AccessChk from Ch5 is how you find weak service DACLs."),
("sa","Where are services defined in the registry?",
 "HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services - one subkey per service, with values like ImagePath, Start, Type and ObjectName (the account).",
 "Added key point linking Ch6 and Ch8."),
]))
